course: money
lesson: 17
title: Loot boxes and "free" skins
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A loot box is a paid mystery prize in a game: you pay, it opens, and you only find out afterwards what you got, which is usually something common.
explain: Jake pays £2 for a box, gets a sword he already has, says "one more go" and opens 5 more: 6 boxes cost him £12 for one rare item, because the hope of the next box keeps you buying.
explain: "Rare skin: 1% chance" means 1 in 100: on average you would need about 100 tries, which at £1 each is about £100, and you could be unlucky for much longer.
explain: Many experts say loot boxes work like gambling, because you pay money and hope; some countries have limited or banned them, it was reported, while game companies say they are just surprises like a card pack, so people disagree.
explain: "Free" skins are often paid for with your time watching ads, and YouTubers who open 50 boxes often got them free and edit the video to show only the lucky bits.
fact: A loot box is a paid mystery prize in a game; you do not know what is inside until after you pay.
fact: Gambling means risking money in the hope of winning something worth more.
fact: Many experts say loot boxes are like gambling; game companies say they are just surprises; people disagree.
fact: Belgium was reported to ban paid loot boxes, and other countries have argued about limiting them.
fact: A 1% chance means 1 in 100; on average it takes about 100 tries, but it can take far more.
fact: "Free" things in games are often paid for with your time and attention to adverts, which earn the game money.
fact: Videos of people opening many loot boxes are often edited to show only the best results, and the boxes may have been given to them free.
fact: Mystery packs usually give you less than you paid, because most people get common items.
fact: Buying the exact item you want is certain; paying for a chance is not.
fact: Spinning wheels with "win up to" prizes work like fruit machines: the top prize is very rare and most spins win small amounts.
fact: Setting a spending limit before you start helps you stop when hoping makes you want "just one more".
fact: "Gutted" is British slang for very disappointed.
exercise: What is a loot box? | A paid mystery prize in a game | A free chest of treasure for everyone | A box of real toys sent by post | A savings jar inside a game
exercise: Jake opens 6 loot boxes at £2 each. How much does he spend? | £12 | £8 | £6 | £10
exercise: What does a 1% chance mean? | 1 in 100 | 1 in 10 | 1 in 1,000 | 1 in 2
exercise: On average, about how many tries does a 1% chance need? | About 100 | About 10 | About 1 | About 1,000
exercise: At £1 a try, about how much would 100 tries cost? | About £100 | About £10 | About £1 | About £1,000
exercise: Kyle watches 5 ads of 30 seconds for a "free" skin. How many seconds of ads is that? | 150 | 35 | 300 | 50
exercise: What does a game earn when you watch ads for a "free" skin? | Money from the advertisers | Nothing at all, it's completely free | Your bank details and home address | A free game for you to keep forever
exercise: Which country was reported to ban paid loot boxes? | Belgium | Australia | Canada | Japan
exercise: Ollie buys 5 card packs at £2 each with his birthday money. How much does he spend? | £10 | £7 | £5 | £12
exercise: Why do loot-box videos often show amazing results? | They are edited to show only the lucky bits | Everyone always gets rare things if they keep going | YouTubers are just better at opening boxes than us | Games give more rare prizes to people with cameras
exercise: Amira's YouTuber opens 50 boxes at £2 each. How much would that cost you? | £100 | £52 | £50 | £25
exercise: Dan paid £5 for 3 items worth 2 coins each. How many coins are they worth in all? | 6 | 5 | 2 | 3
exercise: Freya buys the exact skin she wants for £4. Why is that safer than 4 boxes at £1? | She knows exactly what she gets | Boxes are always more expensive | Skins in boxes are fake | Buying direct gives you double
exercise: Leo spins a £1 wheel 3 times and wins 5 gems each time. How many gems did he win? | 15 | 5 | 3 | 10,000
exercise: What does "win up to 10,000 gems" really mean? | The top prize is possible but very rare | Everyone who spins wins 10,000 gems at least once | Most spins land on the big 10,000 gem prize | You always win at least 1,000 gems a spin
exercise: Eva opened 8 packs at 50p this week. How much did she spend? | £4 | £8 | 80p | £4.50
exercise: What helps you stop "just one more go"? | Setting a spending limit before you start | Opening the boxes faster so you don't think | Buying the biggest pack of gems first to save money | Watching more unboxing videos to learn tricks
test: Eva goes "I'm not gambling, I'm just opening packs." Her big sis: "if you pay and don't know what you get, that's pretty close, babe." What's fair to say? | Many experts agree with the sister, though companies disagree | Eva is totally right, it's nothing like gambling | Packs are illegal everywhere in the world | Gambling only counts with real coins
test: Kyle: "free my backside, I wasted ages!" ("my backside" means not at all.) What did the skin really cost? | Two and a half minutes of his time watching ads | Absolutely nothing at all, because ads are free to watch | £5 of real money | His password
test: Jake: "rubbish, one more go." He's spent £12 on 6 boxes. What's the trap? | The hope of the next box keeps him paying | The next box is guaranteed to be a rare one, so he keeps going | Each box gets cheaper | He gets his money back after 10
test: Grace's dad explains "1% chance". She says "so if I buy 100, I definitely get it." Is she right? | No, it's about 100 on average, but never certain | Yes, because the 100th box is always the rare one by law | Yes, and maybe twice | No, 1% means it never happens
test: Ollie spent all £10 and didn't get the player. He's gutted. ("Gutted" means very upset.) What could help next time? | Decide a limit first, or buy something certain | Spend £20 next time to be sure of getting the skin | Borrow from friends so he can keep on opening boxes | Never tell anyone how he feels about losing money
test: Amira sighs "why don't I ever get that?" about a YouTuber's 50 lucky boxes. What's likely? | The video shows only the best bits, and boxes may have been free | The YouTuber has a secret code for rare items | Amira is just unlucky for ever | Rare items only go to people over 18
test: Leo's wheel: "Spin for £1! Win up to 10,000 gems!" He spins 3 times and gets 15 gems worth about 15p. How much did he lose? | About £2.85 | About £3.15 | About 15p | Nothing
test: A US cousin goes "loot boxes are mid, just buy the skin, fam." ("Mid" means average or not good; "fam" means mate.) Freya buys her £4 skin. What does she get? | Exactly the skin she wanted, for sure | Maybe the skin, or maybe just a common sword | Four random skins chosen for her by the game | A 1% chance of getting the skin she wanted
test: What would happen if a game had to show the real odds on every box? | Players could see how unlikely rare items are and choose better | Rare items would become common | Boxes would become free | Nothing, odds don't matter
test: Dan's "mystery pack" for £5 had 3 items worth 2 coins each. He shouts "robbed, mate!" Why do packs work like this? | Most people get common items, so the game earns more than it gives | Dan was just very unlucky, packs are usually worth more | The game makes a loss on every pack | Mystery packs always give exactly what you paid
test: Ben's brother reads that some countries limited loot boxes. Mum says "good." Your mate says "nah, they're just like a card pack." What's the honest answer? | People disagree; some call it gambling, companies call it a surprise | Everyone agrees loot boxes are fine | Everyone agrees loot boxes are illegal everywhere | Loot boxes don't exist any more
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a "loot box"? | A purchasable article within a game, the contents of which are unknown prior to payment | A receptacle for the storage of coins within the home | A prize furnished without charge to every player | A parcel of physical goods dispatched by post
exam: It is given that a desired article is obtainable with a probability of one per cent per attempt, each attempt costing one pound. Determine the approximate expenditure required on average. | One hundred pounds | Ten pounds | One pound | One thousand pounds
exam: It is given that six mystery boxes are purchased at two pounds each. Compute the total expenditure. | Twelve pounds | Eight pounds | Ten pounds | Six pounds
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the regulation of loot boxes. | Certain countries have reportedly restricted them, and opinion remains divided | All countries have prohibited them without exception | No government has ever considered their regulation | They are universally regarded as harmless
